# Sprite Foundry — Starship Counter Drydock

> Companion tool for **Star Trek: Tactical Command** · single-file, offline · pipeline: **extract → orient → bake**

The Sprite Foundry turns a **scanned ship counter** (the printed hex token — ship art on a tile, usually with a white ID number) into a clean, transparent, correctly-oriented **256×256 PNG sprite** ready to drop into the game. It also emits the small code entry you paste into the game's sprite table.

Everything runs in the browser from one HTML file — no install, no server, no internet. Just open `sprite_foundry.html`.

> **Why it matters going forward:** this is the front end for adding new ships, and it's the natural basis for the planned **custom ship construction** feature — players will use this same extract-orient-bake flow to bring their own hulls into the game.

---

## Before you scan (this is the whole ballgame)

The extractor's hardest problem is that **the darkest hull tones and a near-black counter tile can be the same color** — so a careless scan makes any "remove the background" step eat real hull. Capture well and the rest is easy:

- **Scan at high resolution** (~1200 DPI is what the reference set used) with **blacks not crushed** — let the darkest hull sit a bit above pure black so it separates from the tile.
- Keep the **white paper margins** around each counter — the tool uses that light border as a segmentation cue.
- **Don't** put black paper behind the tiles; it removes the white cue and doesn't help.
- Multiple ships in one scan is fine — you crop them one at a time.

See `FOUNDRY_HANDOVER.md` for the measured details behind this.

