## The workflow

### Step 1 — Load counter scans
Drop your scan(s) onto the loader or click to browse. Each becomes a thumbnail; the first one loads into the source view automatically. Click any thumbnail to switch to it.

### Step 2 — Crop & extract the ship
**Drag a box** around a single counter in the Source view. The extracted ship appears live in the right-hand preview. Two helpers: **Auto-detect hex** (guesses a crop from the dark art) and **Use whole image**.

Then tune the cleanup:

- **Processing** — **Raw (untouched)** *[default]* floods the background away from the crop border and keeps every ship pixel exactly as scanned (one clean resize happens only at bake). **Full pipeline** is the older gate-based cleanup for dark counter sheets. *Start with Raw.*
- **Strip white counter number** — removes the printed ID digit from the tile.
- **Background polarity** — **Auto** / **Dark bg** / **Light bg**. Auto reads the crop border; override if it guesses wrong.
- **Sample background colour (Eyedropper 🎯)** — for mottled/concrete tiles, click a background spot on the source to key off that exact colour. **Clear** to remove the sample.
- **Background match tolerance** (0–180, default 70) — how close a pixel must be to the keyed background to be removed. Higher = removes more.
- **Background darkness cutoff** (0–255, default 42) — pixels darker than this read as background (dark-tile mode). Raise carefully — too high eats dark hull.
- **Kill bright scan debris — brightness** (80–255, default 150) and **— max colour** (0–120, default 45) — together strip bright, near-grey scanner speckle without touching saturated ship art.
- **Saturation boost** (1.0×–2.6×) / **Contrast boost** (1.0×–2.0×) — lift pale token art.
- **Smoothing** (0–3, default 1) — dissolves halftone print grain; also sets the pre-bake downscale target.
- **Erode stray speckles** *(on by default)* — removes lone leftover pixels.

Goal: the ship's real silhouette, nothing else — no tile leak, no eaten hull, no confetti.

### Step 3 — Orient the bow to the **F** arc
The game draws every ship **nose-up** and rotates it by facing, so the sprite's **bow must point at ▲ F (forward/up)**. Use the **Rotation** slider (0–359°, counter-clockwise) and the **−15 / −5 / −1 / +1 / +5 / +15** nudge buttons to line the bow up with the F marker on the orientation hex.

### Step 4 — Bake
Enter a **Sprite key** (the class placeholder, e.g. `V30WingedDefender`) and click **⚙ Bake sprite**. You get:

- The final **256×256 sprite** preview.
- A **drop-in code entry** — paste it beside the others in the game's sprite table (`SHIP_SPRITES`); the accompanying META line reserves the name-plate spot.
- **Copy code** and **Download PNG** buttons.

